WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.103
Gentlemen, my favorite
activities are camping,

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.772
pool parties, and picnics,

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:08.675
but you can't have any of those
things without a barbecue.

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:11.344
In this first round
of competition,

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:13.680
you'll have to harvest steel
from this barbecue

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:15.415
<i> and forge camp knives</i>

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.516
<i> between ten
 and 12 inches in length</i>

00:00:17.616 --> 00:00:19.753
<i> as measured from the tip
 to the plunge grind.</i>

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.620
And one other thing.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.390
Your blades must also include...

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:26.559
an operational bottle opener.

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:30.697
Good luck, Bladesmiths.
Your time starts...

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.667
now.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.437
Immediately, I look at the spit

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:38.838
<i> and that looks to me</i>

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.473
like it's gonna
be high-carbon steel.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.609
Put it down. Put it down
a little lower past this.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:46.646
Now it's up to their own
skill set to determine

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.548
what's mild steel,
what's high-carbon steel,

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:53.386
and how to assemble those
things into a billet.

00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:56.056
Well, let's test some
of these pieces of steel here.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:58.958
You can do a spark test.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:03.095
But a surefire way
is take a piece of steel,

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:05.131
get it hot, quench it,
and check it.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.499
[Cameron]<i> I chop off
 a little piece of that spit,</i>

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:09.201
<i> I throw it in
 the forge immediately</i>

00:01:09.268 --> 00:01:11.937
to see if I can get it
up to temp, quench it,

00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:14.941
and check to see if it's hard.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.944
As that piece gets up to heat,
I quench it...

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:20.547
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:23.683
No.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:26.552
The file does not skate at all,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.688
<i> which means that that's not
 hardenable steel.</i>

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:31.057
<i> I need to think of a plan B.</i>

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.226
Ding. Light bulb goes off.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:34.593
<i> Throw it in a canister.</i>

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:37.229
<i> I'm just gonna cut up
 pieces of steel</i>

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.166
<i> and I'm gonna throw all of it
 into a canister with 1095,</i>

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.968
<i> and that will hopefully
 give me a solid billet.</i>

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:44.970
If you're not sure
what's in there,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.706
I'd go with canister Damascus.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.241 line:0%
You got a 1095 carbon steel

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:50.376 line:0%
you know you can mix
in between.

00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:55.748
Once my billet is up
to the right temperature,

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.351
I'm gonna
very gently squeeze it.

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.287
Just think of it
as like a snowball.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:03.389
You push too hard,
it's gonna go everywhere.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.358
You slowly pack it both ways,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.192
<i> and after you get
 to where you need to be,</i>

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:08.427
<i> you can get some force on.</i>

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.130
<i> First you need
 to take baby steps</i>

00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:12.866
to get that weld set.

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.503
It's now or never.
I gotta get this welded.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:18.671
Oh no.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.409
Oh, come on, man.

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:25.444
What happened to the canister?

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:29.382
[Justin]<i> Big old gap in there.
 This is bad.</i>

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:31.284
[bleep]!

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.720
[Cameron]
<i> As I peel off the canister,</i>

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:36.122
I am just hoping

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.758
that I have a solid weld.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.130
Oh no.

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:45.965
-[Wil] Oh!
-What? What?

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:48.268
Wow. I dropped it
when I was grinding,

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:49.969
and it split on me.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.671
He's inspecting
the inner contents,

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:55.208
to see if they welded up,
and he doesn't look happy.

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:57.376
[David] Again, when
he came off the press,

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.179
it didn't look like
he'd reduced that can much.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:01.914
He might not have
pressed it down.

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:03.983
[Cameron]
<i> This is unusable steel.</i>

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.651
[Ben] Check out Justin.

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:08.988
I think he's thinking about
what to do now.

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:10.456
I have to start over.

00:03:11.423 --> 00:03:14.593
Now I'm moving on
to that big chunk of spit.

00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:16.396
Just taking the best guess
on what I know

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:18.831
I can at least get
a nice-shaped object out of.

00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:24.437
I cut off pieces of the grate.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.074
All right, round two.
Here we go.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.842
I know that
I'm up against the clock,

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.645
<i> and it's gonna be tight, it's
 gonna come down to the wire,</i>

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:33.880
but I never give up.

00:03:35.614 --> 00:03:38.685
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:42.054
We got Nick trying to bang
a billet out of a hot can.

00:03:42.487 --> 00:03:43.990
It's not working out for him.

00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:46.759
[Nick]<i> It is very difficult.</i>

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:50.230
Luckily, the can
finally comes off.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.799
-Woo.
-There we go.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:56.236
[Justin]<i> I've never put a bottle
 opener in a blade before.</i>

00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:59.338
<i> I'm gonna put a ring
 at the back end of the knife.</i>

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:01.740
I think that swell will help
keep the knife in your hand,

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:04.510
<i> and it's not up on the blade
 in case you have to baton it,</i>

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:06.011
and you're not
gonna cut yourself

00:04:06.078 --> 00:04:08.647
when you're opening your beer
when you're camping or fishing.

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.916
[Wil] Justin was
able to quickly forge out

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:12.451
a blade with his mono steel

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:15.821
and Cameron's still working
on his second can.

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:17.523
[Doug] He's still got
a long ways to go.

00:04:17.656 --> 00:04:19.525
We gotta heat treat this thing.

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:27.199
Nick's blade is looking nice.
That's a nice camp knife shape.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:28.668
My blade is fully hardened.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.339
Justin just quenched.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:35.474
[Justin]<i> I'm just praying
 to the gods of metal</i>

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:37.644
that this blade
is gonna come out hard.

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.680
It's not hard.

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.449
<i> I think, "Well, maybe
 I'm working with mild steel."</i>

00:04:44.549 --> 00:04:45.985
If I don't have a hard blade,

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:47.253
I might get eliminated.

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:48.787
<i> I'm freaking out.</i>

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:51.290
I don't have time to make
a whole 'nother blade.

00:04:51.723 --> 00:04:54.727
All I need to do is have
a high-carbon edge.

00:04:55.494 --> 00:04:57.196
<i> I'm gonna cut
 some pieces off this grate,</i>

00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:58.731
and I'm gonna do a snap test.

00:04:58.865 --> 00:05:00.299
<i> I know if I hit that
 son of a bitch</i>

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:01.867
with a hammer
and it breaks,

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:03.602
that means it has carbon
in it and it got hard

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:05.638
and I can forge weld
it onto the edge.

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:09.708
-Broke it right off.
-Broke it right off.

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:12.378
[Wil] Now you know
that's hardened steel.

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:15.114
[Cameron]<i> I'm able to peel
 my second canister off</i>

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.081
and start forging it.

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:18.684
[Wil] Cameron is so far behind

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:19.885
the power curve.

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.921
[Cameron]<i> I know that
 to meet the parameters,</i>

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:24.723
I still have to put the bottle
opener on this knife.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.526
[Wil] Cameron needs
to work very quickly

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:29.462
because he still
has to quench that blade.

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:33.098
Justin is perfectly shaping

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:35.901
that piece of high-carbon steel

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.402
to the edge of his blade.

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:39.105
I really do applaud the fact

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.107
that he was
in the middle of that

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.476
and found a way out.
I like it.

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:47.947
[Justin]<i> The clock's ticking.
 I'm almost out of time.</i>

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:52.017
The high-carbon steel on
my edge needs to get hard.

00:05:52.117 --> 00:05:55.387
[dramatic music playing]

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:57.090
And here comes the file test.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:05:59.992
[bleep] yeah, man. It's hard.

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:00.826
Thirty seconds!

00:06:00.926 --> 00:06:03.730
You got enough.
You got enough, buddy.

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:06.465
-Thank you!
-There you go!

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:07.700
[Ben] You got it.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.169
Five, four, three,

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:11.837
two, one!

00:06:11.937 --> 00:06:15.207
This first round
of competition is over.

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:16.542
All right, Bladesmiths.

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:19.178
This is the sharpness test,
the pork shoulder slice.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:21.847
Unlike the strength test,
this is all about

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:24.350
what your edge will do
to these pork shoulders.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:26.686
Cameron, you're up first.
Are you ready?

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:27.820
Yes, please.

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:29.789
[dramatic music playing]

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:34.660
Since we asked you
to do a bottle opener,

00:06:34.793 --> 00:06:36.195
we're gonna test if it'll open.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:38.264
Just to let you know,
it's root beer,

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:40.032
because we don't beer and blade.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.468
[chuckling]

00:06:43.535 --> 00:06:46.806
[dramatic music playing]

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:01.253
All right, Cameron, first up.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.387
Your bottle opener works.

00:07:02.854 --> 00:07:06.192
Your edge, it's not
very sharp, but it cuts.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.861
But that's a lot also to do
with the velocity I'm doing

00:07:08.927 --> 00:07:11.196
in chopping through
the meat right there.

00:07:11.697 --> 00:07:13.132
With everything
that you've done,

00:07:13.532 --> 00:07:15.835
your blade, sir, it'll cut.

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:18.637
Justin, it's your turn.

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.372
-Are you ready, sir?
-Oh yeah, bud.

00:07:20.472 --> 00:07:24.444
[dramatic music playing]

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:38.090
Geez.

00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:40.592
All right, Justin.

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:44.463
Your edge not only is sharp,
but look at what it did.

00:07:44.563 --> 00:07:47.032
It cut through bone.
It's a deep cutter.

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:49.034
And of course...

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:50.169
I got a swig.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.773
Overall, sir, your blade,
it will cut.

00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:54.840
Thank you.

00:07:55.407 --> 00:07:57.075
Nick, your turn, sir.
Are you ready?

00:07:57.209 --> 00:07:58.777
-Let's do it.
-Let's do this.

00:07:58.877 --> 00:08:01.747
[dramatic rock music playing]

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:06.052
I'm thirsty!

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:07.720
And... ah!

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.756
I guess I'm gonna
do this test thirsty.

00:08:19.965 --> 00:08:21.167
-Nice.
-Thanks.

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:23.401
All right, Nick.

00:08:23.502 --> 00:08:25.771
It's very nice,
it slice and dice.

00:08:25.871 --> 00:08:28.908
Nice slashes, nice deep cuts.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:31.176
Even cuts to the bone.

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:32.377
But I'm still thirsty.

00:08:32.878 --> 00:08:36.048
Overall, sir, your blade,
it will cut.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:37.216
Yes.

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:40.519
All right, gents, it's time
for one of you Bladesmiths

00:08:40.619 --> 00:08:41.954
to leave the Forge.

00:08:42.054 --> 00:08:44.190
The Bladesmith
leaving the Forge is...

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:47.426
Cameron.

00:08:47.526 --> 00:08:48.827
Your blade did not make the cut.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:50.829
You did a great job,
but at this time,

00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:53.131
I need you to please
surrender your blade.

00:08:54.132 --> 00:08:57.435
[Cameron]<i> I'm proud
 that I never really gave up.</i>

00:08:57.536 --> 00:08:58.971
-Good job, Cam. Seriously.
-Thanks.

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:01.573
I believe that I gave
everything I had

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:02.842
<i> through this entire challenge,</i>

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:05.144
and I sure had
a hell of a time competing.

